WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.663
Why start with an inventory?

2
00:00:01.663 --> 00:00:04.576
Because the list is always
longer than you think.

3
00:00:04.576 --> 00:00:10.032
In Entra, consent creates service principals,
and Microsoft's own services create

4
00:00:10.032 --> 00:00:15.087
more, so the inventory is always larger
than the list of apps anyone asked for.

5
00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:19.663
And in Active Directory, no attribute
marks a user as a service account.

6
00:00:19.663 --> 00:00:25.057
Microsoft Learn's guidance on securing
user-based service accounts finds candidates

7
00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:27.529
by three signs: a service principal name,

8
00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:30.975
trust for delegation, and a
password that never expires.

9
00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:33.400
And it warns that this
might not find them all.

10
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.254
So in this module you read every
non-person identity into one register,

11
00:00:38.254 --> 00:00:43.275
with its type, its owners, its credentials,
its risk and a candidate target.

12
00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:45.200
You don't decide anything yet.

13
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.830
Inventory first; decide later.
